---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../content';
const path = "/";
const title = "Choose Shopify email apps around the work your store needs";
const desc = "A practical map for evaluating email apps, comparing costs, and building Shopify lifecycle workflows.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which app is best overall?",
    "answer": "There is no demonstrated universal winner here. Compare candidates against your mandatory workflow, cost forecast and operator rehearsal, including the incumbent."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do I need both email and SMS?",
    "answer": "Only add a channel with a defined audience, permission source, owner and measurement plan. Model its additional costs and cross-channel suppression."
  },
  {
    "question": "How quickly should I migrate?",
    "answer": "Use acceptance checks and team capacity to set the schedule. Verify identity, consent, sender ownership, rollback and reporting before expanding."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <p class="editorial-kicker">Shopify email operating library</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Find the customer problem first, then choose the software and operating plan. This library connects app profiles, comparisons, store contexts and workflow guides. It does not present a measured universal winner or claim that the exercises below are completed merchant tests.</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Start with a decision you can name</h2>
      <p>Write the current failure in observable terms: checkout reminders reach buyers after purchase, a new subscriber receives two offers, or finance cannot reconcile campaign revenue. Record the incumbent process and its cost. A missing process may need an owner before it needs another subscription.</p><p>Choose one outcome and one safety condition. For cart recovery that might be a valid return-to-checkout link with purchase suppression. For a newsletter it might be a usable product layout and a reliable approval process. These require different demonstrations.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Use the library by question</h2>
      <ul><li><a href="/apps">App profiles</a>: build a candidate list and collect questions for suppliers.</li><li><a href="/compare">Comparisons</a>: examine two candidates against the same requirements.</li><li><a href="/alternatives">Alternatives</a>: decide what must survive an exit from an incumbent.</li><li><a href="/for">Store contexts</a>: adapt requirements to the catalog and buying cycle.</li><li><a href="/use-cases">Use cases</a>: define the trigger, message, exclusions and reporting.</li><li><a href="/guides">Operator guides</a>: plan selection, migration and measurement.</li></ul><p>A profile or comparison is a starting point for inquiry. Confirm the current plan, integration and billing terms directly before committing; an editorial placement is not proof that a capability works on your store.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Separate capture, sending and customer operations</h2>
      <p>Draw the current stack with an owner for each task: collecting permission, storing identity, sending campaigns, running automations, answering replies and reporting orders. Keep a sender only where it has a defined responsibility. Two tools can coexist when eligibility and pause controls are explicit.</p><p>If capture is weak, adding another sender may leave the bottleneck intact. If support cannot resolve sizing questions, more reminders may increase messages without solving purchase friction. Compare improving the current process with replacing it.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Build a shortlist from mandatory requirements</h2>
      <p>Use <a href="/guides/selection-framework">the selection framework</a> to distinguish mandatory requirements from preferences. Require a demonstration of the exact order state, variant, consent source and subscription context you use. Record each requirement as verified, failed or unresolved rather than turning unknowns into scores.</p><p>Keep the incumbent in the comparison. An attractive editor does not compensate for a missing suppression condition. A specialist may still fit if a documented integration supplies the missing function and the combined cost and ownership are acceptable.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Compare costs on the same horizon</h2>
      <p>Prepare a normal period, a peak period and a growth case using your contact counts, billable identities, sends and channel usage. Include capture tools, SMS, services, migration labor and subscriptions that remain after the change. Record currency, tax treatment and allowances with the quote date.</p><p><a href="/guides/pricing-models">Pricing models</a> explains the calculation. There is no universal order count at which a store should migrate and no fixed stack-cost percentage that proves a tool is wasteful. Compare the forecast with your budget and the work the team can maintain.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Rehearse a complete customer moment</h2>
      <p>Proposed exercise: on non-sending fixtures, create a new subscriber, a purchaser and a cart with an unavailable variant. Ask the operator to edit the audience and offer, inspect who qualifies, preview the message, pause it and restore the prior version. Record the actual steps and unresolved dependencies.</p><p>This is evidence of configuration behavior, not evidence of merchant revenue lift. Use <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery</a> for event and inventory checks and <a href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a> for a report that can detect missing or duplicated data.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Adapt to the buying cycle</h2>
      <p>Subscription stores need to distinguish renewals from new purchases; wholesale stores may need account-level ownership; fashion stores need variant and size accuracy. A gift purchase does not necessarily reveal the purchaser’s own product preference. Write these distinctions into fixtures before comparing interfaces.</p><p>A short promotion and a considered purchase need different content and evaluation windows. Start with your support questions and observed buying intervals rather than copying a universal send calendar.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Move in reversible stages</h2>
      <p>Inventory active senders, record consent and suppression sources, and export definitions before cutover. Assign one sender to each equivalent workflow. Rehearse pause and rollback before enabling the replacement; a parallel evaluation does not require duplicate production messages.</p><p><a href="/guides/migration">Migration</a> and <a href="/guides/implementation-plan">implementation planning</a> cover the handoff. Schedule around your operational capacity and known events. Expand only after the current stage’s identity, eligibility and reporting checks pass.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Read results without inventing a counterfactual</h2>
      <p>Track delivery failures, complaints, clicks, order linkage, discounts, refunds and contribution alongside attributed revenue. A platform crediting an order does not establish that the message caused it. Cohorts describe behavior; properly designed comparisons address causal questions.</p><p>Use <a href="/guides/revenue-attribution">revenue attribution</a> when deciding what a lift claim requires. Keep the supplier decision separate from the message experiment: a migration and an offer change at the same time cannot isolate which change mattered.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Leave a decision record for the next operator</h2>
      <p>Save the chosen workflow, acceptance evidence, forecast, owner, known gaps and rollback conditions. State why the incumbent remains or why the replacement solves a verified problem. Review when requirements, pricing or operational evidence changes, rather than migrating on a recurring deadline.</p><p>The useful output of this homepage is a scoped next action: inspect a profile, compare finalists, rehearse a workflow or repair the current process. Reading every page is not a prerequisite for collecting that evidence.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
